From: Magnus Lindholm <linmag7@gmail.com>
Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/1] PCI/MSI: notify drivers of permanent interrupt delivery loss

Provide a sleepable notification for permanently contained MSI delivery
loss and a separate software-only failure handoff. The generic API applies
independently of SPARC; its tg3 consumer and Oberon caller are separate.

The controller blocks new allocations across driver rebinding and retains
resources that may still receive writes. The core holds the device lock
for each callback, but not across the pair of calls. Drivers must inspect
the current binding's state and preserve an already-legacy replacement.

Recovery takes a runtime-PM reference; failure notification does not resume
the device. That callback marks software unavailable without initiating
hardware access. It does not prove that existing work or DMA has stopped.
No helper imposes a callback timeout or retries a failed notification.

PCI error recovery coordinates channel errors and reset/resume. Here the
endpoint can remain usable while its host MSI service is permanently lost.
The current tg3 error path restores saved PCI state and restarts the
existing setup; it does not release vectors and select legacy interrupts.
Allocation blocking alone cannot convert an already-open interface, and
controller-driven vector freeing would bypass driver teardown ownership.

We therefore propose explicit driver notification. Recovery may access
hardware; the separate failure method cannot initiate hardware accesses
when resume or recovery failed. The documentation compares allocation
blocking, PCI error recovery, notifier and forced-unbind alternatives.
PCI feedback on a dedicated API versus equivalent error-recovery extensions
is requested before the dependent hardware support can be merged.

The combined eight-CPU M3000 test allocated 12 MSI-X vectors across all
four onboard BCM5718 ports. GLAN0 and GLAN2 passed concurrent hashed
8 MiB transfers in both directions and reopen testing. Exhausting the
256 lifetime IDs moved spare ports to legacy IRQs while management kept
its MSI-X vectors; both paths passed further transfers with taint zero.

A separate diagnostic image stopped the shared MSI queue. All four
callbacks completed, all 12 vectors were released and the ports reopened
on legacy IRQs. Both connected ports passed further transfers. This was
a queue-stop test, not an actual PCIe error or queue-overflow injection.

UP/SMP W=1 links passed; UP was not booted. Earlier x86-64 PM-enabled
PCI/tg3 object builds and MSI-disabled tg3 builds also passed. Existing
unrelated warnings in initial full builds remain. The API review changed
only comments and documentation; no new full-kernel build or boot is
claimed. Twenty-one extracted API cases passed with ASan/UBSan, checking
outcomes, mock lock/PM accounting and dispatch to a replacement binding.
These are sequential mocked services, not runtime-PM or lockdep tests.

Actual runtime-PM, AER, hot removal and arbitrary drivers are not qualified.
The callback API and permanent-retention policy require subsystem review.
Codex assisted with implementation, source review and test preparation.
